{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AGENTS.md GLOSSARY.md docs/planning/review-protocol.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 agent guidance\n\n## Current stage\n\nP01–P07 completed. The owner-authorized P09 integration proceeds under D058/D059\nwhile P08/C26 remains open. C44 attempt-05 passed at 9 in all dimensions and is\nmerged; actual L19 wizard verification passed. C29 attempt-07 was accepted at 8,\nand its restored native coverage must run in the Worf VM before the C29–C32 and\nP09 re-reviews. Read [WORF-START-HERE](docs/planning/WORF-START-HERE.md),\n[status](docs/planning/status.md) and the exact retained verdicts for current state.\nThe approved [C45/P10 brief](docs/planning/C45-draft.md) starts after P09 passes at\n9. Do not re-ask its settled naming, fresh-setup or sequencing decisions.\n\nProduct endpoints are called only within explicit controlled live scope. L19’s\noriginal two plus separately approved third reads are all charged; no additional\nL19 live call is authorized. The next fixture gates need no real credentials.\nDo not copy host Keychains, private manifests, CLI tenant TOMLs or auth profiles.\n\n## Accepted direction\n\n- React presentation hosted by Tauri; focused Rust application implementation.\n- Use the Rust SCM SDK directly; no shipped Node/Bun backend runtime or\n  TypeScript backend workers. Build tooling and React WebView JavaScript are\n  distinct from a shipped backend runtime.\n- Academy owns its learner presentation and hosted learning contracts. Share its\n  React components/themes/shell where suitable; preserve hosted grading/access\n  authority. Academy gamification stays in the Academy feature.\n- Selectively evaluate cdot-framework modules and architecture patterns. Do not\n  carry media generation, commercial billing, MySQL/Redis, or its server stack\n  into the desktop runtime. Preserve upstream provenance/licenses.\n- Cover working v1 behavior in testable slices; classify previews/stubs explicitly.\n- macOS Apple Silicon first; v2 coexists with v1 using independent application,\n  protected-storage, and local-data identities.\n- Rust owns credential access, tenant context, request authority, and reviewed\n  configuration changes. Harness proposals do not authorize Apply.\n- D060 removes alpha import from the one presentation. C45/P10 removes the\n  remaining alpha readers/contracts and starts the new namespace with fresh\n  setup; no alpha/v2 data, credentials or approvals are migrated.\n- Offline local work is supported; no silent replay of remote operations.\n- Remote Gateway usage/cost/budget/limit administration is product functionality,\n  not desktop application monetization.\n\n## Source ownership and working safety\n\n- This repository: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.\n- Framework reference: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/ampersand-services`.\n- V1 behavioral reference: `/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-desktop`.\n- Shared Academy: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/ai-security-academy`.\n- SDK: `airs.coder:/home/cdot/development/cdot65/scm-sdk-rs`.\n- Desktop-capable native Harness source:\n  `/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-harness-desktop-mcp`.\n\nRead applicable guidance before changing another checkout. Preserve active SDK\nwork and user changes. Pin accepted external revisions for reproducible inputs;\ndo not assume evolving worktrees or an older similarly named Harness checkout\nmatch the required contracts. Current research evidence is a snapshot, not live\nqualification. Never read or log credentials for planning.\n\n## Reviews and completion\n\nMost original implementation was Claude Code; the owner authorized Codex for\nthe current integration and remediation. Under D026, every implementation chunk and every phase\n(cumulatively) requires a real independent Codex review scoring at least 9/10 in\narchitecture, behavior, authority_security, validation and overall, with empty\nrequired_fixes, blockers and evidence_gaps. Historical G00/G01 Claude reviews\nare retained unchanged and cover only their recorded inputs.\nUnder D034 (superseding D032 for chunks) a chunk completes on a review scoring\nat least 8 in all five dimensions with no blockers (`xtask complete\n--owner-accept REASON`); the ledger records the acceptance and every accepted\nfailure (required fixes, evidence gaps). Phase reviews still need 9/10 with no\nowner acceptance, and every chunk of the phase accepted below 9 must first be\nremediated and re-reviewed to a passing 9/10 (a new generation, later chunks\nnot reopened); `xtask complete` enforces both.\nRetain source/dependency fingerprints, raw verdicts, commands, test results, and\nremediation attempts. Re-review changed source. Never fabricate a score, substitute\nself-review, edit a verdict, or treat favorable scores as evidence that unrun tests\npassed. A queue acknowledgement or process exit status is not a verdict.\n\nFollow `docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`, the ordered C01–C26 instructions,\nreview/test/dependency/release contracts and canonical prompts. G01 specifies\nconcrete interfaces and exact native/check commands before implementation.\nDo not invent these future gates as completed results.\nKeep sensitive raw evidence private and repository records redacted.\n\nThe owner specified `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git` as\nthis repository's destination. Use normal pushes only when publication is within\nthe active task; do not force-push, rewrite unrelated repositories, promote v2\nover v1, or publish application releases during planning.\n# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 domain vocabulary\n\nThese terms name the desktop product and its native authority concepts.\nMechanisms, wire formats and transition rules belong in the selected design.\n\n## Language\n\n**Tenant profile**: An application-owned configuration for a Prisma AIRS tenant,\nincluding its label, endpoints, and protected credential references. A scan-only\nprofile does not require management OAuth credentials. A profile is not an\nAcademy learner account or a Gateway workspace.\n\n**Credential capability**: The specific operations a credential can authorize,\nsuch as scanning, management, inference, or workspace MCP. Possessing one\ncapability does not establish another; configuration keeps them explicit.\n\n**Gateway workspace**: A remote AI Gateway administrative/inference context within\nthe selected tenant profile. It is distinct from a local filesystem project.\n\n**Configuration review**: An operator-visible proposal for an AIRS configuration\nchange, bound to the current tenant, credential context, target resource, and\ndraft. Rust owns its validity. Displaying or importing a proposal does not grant\npermission to apply it.\n\n**Remote outcome**: What is known about an attempted remote action: not sent,\nrejected, completed as established by evidence, or unknown. Local timeout,\ncancellation, and disconnection do not establish remote rollback.\n\n**Academy feature**: The learner experience within the broader desktop app.\nPresentation is shared with Academy; remote learning contracts remain hosted.\nLearning progression, XP, cosmetic credits, awards, and other gamification stay\nwithin this feature.\n\n**Academy learner account**: The remote learning identity owning progress,\nassessments, certificates, and account-scoped local drafts/downloads. It is\nindependent of the selected Prisma AIRS tenant and Harness inference sign-in.\n\n**Shared presentation**: React controls, themes, and shell layout reused from the\nAcademy-owned source. Sharing appearance does not share learner authority,\ngamification effects, manager routes, or browser-session assumptions globally.\n\n**Harness**: The assistant feature using the native Rust agent engine and\ndesktop-owned lifecycle, credential grants, reviews, and protected history.\nThe SCM SDK is a separate dependency, not the Harness engine.\n\n**Project scope**: The explicitly selected local filesystem scope and approved\ntool permissions available to a Harness conversation. Preserve the existing\nrestrictions initially; a transcript is not permission to restore approvals.\n\n**MCP grant**: A desktop-owned authorization context for a specific remote MCP\nbinding. Inference sign-in is separate. Discovery does not establish execution;\nexecution evidence must correspond to the actual approved call.\n\n**Historical conversation**: Saved user work that can be inspected after import.\nResumption requires separate context/protocol compatibility validation. It never\nrestores pending approvals or old authentication sessions.\n\n**Billing**: Desktop commercial charging/monetization machinery, excluded from\nv2. Remote Gateway usage/cost/budget/limit administration remains product\nfunctionality. Academy's earned learning economy remains Academy-local.\n\n**Shipped backend runtime**: Executables/workers required by the installed app's\nbackend. Node/Bun and TypeScript backend workers are excluded. Build-time JS\ntooling and React JavaScript executing in the WebView are separate concepts.\n\n**Parity**: Coverage of agreed working v1 behavior with new v2 evidence. V1 source\nimplementation, retained fixture/contract results, retained live evidence, and\nv2 installed-artifact results are distinct. Replacing a preview/stub with a live\nfeature is scope expansion, not implicit parity work.\n\n**Authority principal**: The native-established operator or restricted Harness\nidentity on whose behalf desktop work is requested. A claimed identity in a\nrequest is not an Authority principal.\n\n**Authority handle**: A principal-scoped entry to native desktop operations and\nobservations. Possessing an operation reference does not expand its authority.\n\n**Intent**: A typed request for a specific desktop action under the caller's\ncurrent authority. It is distinct from a Harness proposal or an arbitrary remote\nrequest.\n\n**Receipt**: Native acknowledgement that an Intent was admitted, with its request\nor operation reference. It is not evidence of operator confirmation or remote\ncompletion.\n\n**Refusal**: Native rejection of an Intent before admission under the requested\nauthority. A remote action's failure is a Remote outcome instead.\n\n**ViewScope**: The product context a caller asks to observe, such as a learner\naccount, tenant workspace or Harness conversation. Native authority determines\nwhich part is visible to that caller.\n\n**Observation**: A scoped redacted view of native desktop state and operation\nprogress. It confers no execution or credential authority.\n\n**ContextRef**: A native reference to the particular product context presented\nto the caller. Returning to the same tenant or account does not revive retired\nauthority.\n\n**Delivery session**: The current native-registered channel for one caller's\nIntent deliveries. It is separate from tenant, learner or inference identity.\n\n**Delivery sequence**: The order identity of an Intent within its Delivery session,\nused to distinguish duplicate delivery from an explicit new action.\n_Avoid_: Business retry, authorization token\n\n**Native-owned payload reference**: A scoped reference to staged desktop input\nwhose ownership and content are known natively. It is not permission to read an\narbitrary filesystem path.\n\n**Reviewed**: A Configuration review whose captured proposal remains eligible for\noperator confirmation.\n\n**Confirming**: A Configuration review currently awaiting the native operator's\nanswer for that specific proposal.\n\n**Consumed**: A Configuration review already committed to its single admitted\noperation. Failure of that operation does not make the review reusable.\n\n**Queued**: An admitted desktop operation whose remote dispatch has not been\nclaimed.\n\n**DispatchClaimed**: A desktop operation for which remote dispatch may have begun.\nLocal cancellation at this stage does not prove NotSent.\n\n**Settled**: A desktop operation with a recorded terminal local result and its\ncurrent Remote outcome. Later reconciliation may refine knowledge of that outcome.\n\n**Harness grant**: Native authority for one verified inference identity and its\napproved tenant/workspace/origin context. Rotating that identity's token material\nis distinct from replacing or revoking the Harness grant.\n\n**Grant epoch**: The particular lifetime of a native grant's authority. Retired\nauthority stays retired even when an equivalent identity is selected again.\n\n**One-time secret receipt**: The operator-scoped acknowledgement holding newly\nissued secret material for its explicit transient reveal. It is separate from a\nstored credential lookup.\n\n**Editor lease**: The hold on the reviewed document while native operator\nconfirmation is pending. A changed document or context invalidates that hold.\n# Independent review and evidence protocol\n\nApplies to G00/G01 as planning/design reviews, every implementation chunk and\nevery phase. Scope the verdict honestly: a plan review evaluates the plan and\nfuture gates, not application code or tests that do not exist.\n\nG00/G01 were reviewed by Claude Code; those records are historical and cover\nonly their recorded inputs. Under D026, Claude Code implements and Codex is the\nindependent reviewer for C01–C26 and the phase reviews P01–P08.\n\n## Gate\n\nActual independent Codex review must score at least 9/10 overall and in each\ncritical dimension: architecture, behavior, authority_security, validation.\nNo blocking or required fixes may remain. Missing mandatory evidence, stale\ninputs, or a mismatched receipt prevents completion independently of scores.\n\nThe implementer cannot substitute its own score or edit the reviewer's verdict.\nDo not ask a reviewer to raise a score; fix findings and request a new review.\nRetain all attempts and explain remediation. Optional suggestions may remain\nonly when explicitly classified as nonrequired and consistent with the plan.\n\n## Dimensions\n\n- **Architecture:** depth at domain interfaces; locality; justified seams/real\n  adapters; authoritative ownership; no needless pass-through layers or hidden\n  cross-module coupling. Use the glossary and accepted ADRs.\n- **Behavior:** complete chunk requirements, exact working-v1 subset, correct\n  success/denied/empty/stale/partial/unknown outcomes, and no invented support.\n- **Authority/security:** context/credential isolation, review/approval binding,\n  trusted hosted assessment, protected storage, safe external destinations,\n  bounded execution, provenance, Node-free shipped backend, and secret hygiene.\n- **Validation:** meaningful production-interface tests; independent external\n  expectations; required native/live/artifact evidence; reproducibility, hashes,\n  explicit limitations, and causal failure cases. Test counts alone earn no score.\n\nScores below 9 require concrete findings with locations, impact, and acceptance\nconditions. Even a 9/10 verdict with a required fix cannot pass.\n\nOwner rule D034 (2026-10-18): a chunk may complete on a review scoring at least\n8 in every dimension with no blockers; its remaining findings are recorded as\naccepted failures. The reviewer still scores against the 9/10 gate above. At\nthe end of each phase every chunk accepted below 9 is remediated and\nre-reviewed until it passes the full gate, before the phase review.\n\n## Phase reviews\n\nAfter the last chunk of a phase completes, request a cumulative Codex review of\nthat phase (P01: C01–C03, P02: C04–C07, P03: C08–C12, P04: C13–C15, P05: C16–C18,\nP06: C19–C22, P07: C23–C24, P08: C25–C26). The phase packet binds the current\ncommitted tree, every chunk receipt in the phase and earlier phases' receipts,\nthe phase's integrated test/native results rerun against that tree, and the\ndiff since the previous phase. The reviewer assesses integration across the\nphase and the earlier features it affects. The same five-score threshold and\nempty required_fixes/blockers/evidence_gaps apply, with no owner acceptance\n(D034), and every member chunk must hold a passing 9/10 completion. The next phase does not\nbegin until its predecessor phase review passes. Historical chunk scores are\ninputs to assess, never a substitute.\n\n## Review transport\n\nThe controller writes an immutable request packet (request/attempt IDs, chunk or\nphase, scope, predecessor receipts, source/dependency/build-flag/evidence\nfingerprints, exact commands/results/evidence paths, diff, limitations, the\nverdict schema and one absolute verdict path), then delivers it:\n\n- Preferred: the owner-designated Codex thread, addressed by exact ID through\n  `codex queue --thread <id> --message <text>`; never `--last`. A prior\n  reviewer-authored handshake file proves routing. Queue success is submission\n  only. The controller waits with bounded progress for the matching verdict.\n- Fallback: a fresh `codex exec --sandbox read-only --ignore-user-config\n  --ignore-rules --ephemeral` reviewer with web search disabled, approval\n  policy never, `--output-schema` and captured JSONL stdout/stderr. It is\n  recorded as a fresh reviewer under a new attempt; the prior attempt is\n  expired so a late verdict cannot advance the gate. No bypass flags.\n\nThe verdict must validate against the strict schema, echo the request ID and\nsupplied fingerprints, and the frozen inputs must be unchanged afterwards.\n\n## Receipt fields\n\nRecord review ID, chunk/gate/phase ID, attempt, request ID, transport (thread\nID or fresh exec), time, actual harness/version/model when reported, input prompt hash, source fingerprint, dependency manifest hash,\ncheck/evidence manifest hash, raw output hash, reviewer scores, findings,\nrequired fixes, blocking findings, limitations, and whether source stayed frozen.\nFor final packages also bind pre-sign/post-sign/stapled artifact hashes and the\ninstalled-byte inventory. A review of a different tree or package cannot pass.\n\nThe input fingerprint includes tracked/nonignored source, instruction files,\nconfiguration, file modes/symlink targets, and exact compiled dependency inputs.\nDocument narrow exclusions for review outputs/mutable status to avoid circular\nhashes. Exclusions cannot hide application sources, lockfiles, generated bindings,\nbuild flags, fixture rules, tests, notices, or package inventories. Evidence and\nstatus hashes remain independently recorded even when excluded from source hash.\n\nUse actual Codex review. The fresh fallback runs read-only, ephemeral, without\nuser configuration/rules, web search, hooks or connectors; the controller verifies\nthe effective settings it can observe rather than assuming the sandbox disables\nevery tool. The preferred thread's own sandbox is not controlled by this project;\nits read-only obligation is instructed and enforced by unchanged pre/post\nfingerprints. The reviewer may write only its designated review artifacts.\nThe reviewer reads project guidance and the exact scoped files/evidence deliberately. Evidence hashes detect\nstale/tampered records; they are not cryptographic proof of provider authorship.\n\n## Completion and invalidation\n\nAll predecessors must have passing receipts. Checks must be executed against\nthe same inputs and exact intended feature/build flags. Record each result as\npassed, failed, blocked, or not applicable with a reason. An unavailable required\ncheck is blocked, never silently not applicable.\n\nOn a failed gate: keep chunk active, fix findings, rerun affected checks plus\nany regression surface the fix reaches, freeze new inputs, and obtain a fresh\nindependent review. A dependency update reopens affected consumers. Final release\nrevalidates cumulative behavior and source-bound prior evidence; it cannot merely\nsum historical 9s from incompatible versions.\n\nCompletion is followed by a focused commit whose tree is verified against the\nreviewed source. Changes to source after review require a new relevant review.\nGit publication uses normal pushes to the specified v2 remote; no force-push.\n\n## Evidence handling\n\nRepository summaries contain reproducible commands, environments, hashes,\nnonsecret fixture IDs, pass/fail/blocked status, and limitations. Keep real\ncredentials, prompts/responses, tokens, Keychain exports, unredacted traces,\ncustomer records, signing secrets, and signed-in browser captures private.\nRedact output before publication; retain original sensitive evidence in private\noperator storage with a hash reference when appropriate.\n\nThe C01 controller must reject a score8 in any dimension, required fixes despite\nscore9, missing check results, changed dependency/source hashes, edited raw output,\nduplicate/invalid receipt IDs, changed build flags, malformed schemas, and missing\nnative/artifact proof. Completion state is not writable by a reviewer or inferred\nfrom a successful CLI exit code or queue acknowledgement alone. It also rejects\na verdict whose request ID or echoed fingerprints differ from the packet, a\nverdict for an expired attempt, and a phase completion lacking a passing phase\nreview.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
