{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1997,2212p' docs/planning/decisions.md; sed -n '2053,2106p' docs/architecture/DESIGN.md; cat docs/planning/reviews/P09/attempt-02/invocation.json; sed -n '1,65p' docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs/run-checks.sh\"","aggregated_output":"## D050 — Reviewed v1 import: source contract, mapping, receipts and rollback\n\nDecided in C23 (implementer decision for the C23 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Source.** v1 (74754c02) has no whole-application export, so the import\nreads v1's own storage, read only:\n- **Files** under v1's folder: `~/Library/Application Support/Prisma AIRS\n  Desktop/` in production builds; fixture builds use `fixture-v1/` under\n  their data root and never a real v1.\n- **Keychain items** in v1's service `io.cdot.prisma-airs-desktop`,\n  through a type that can only read (no add, change or delete exists). In\n  production builds it is the login Keychain, and macOS asks before each\n  item is read; that prompt is the platform grant for a protected read.\n  Fixture builds read a staged file keychain without prompting and never\n  the login Keychain.\n- Every path component is checked without following links, files are\n  opened `O_NOFOLLOW`, and only exact names are read: digest-named Academy\n  folders and `attempt-<digest>.bin` records. Nothing is created in v1's\n  folder; v1's Academy `.lock` files are never opened.\n\n**Source lock.** v1 holds `.desktop-instance.lock` exclusively while it\nruns. The import takes it shared, never creating it, from the review until\nthe import settles. v1 running is `sourceLocked`, and v1 cannot start\nduring a review. Each item records the SHA-256 of what it was read from.\nBefore an item is written, a file it came from is read again, and changed\nbytes are `sourceChanged`: nothing of it is written.\n\n**Limits.**\n\n| Source | Limit |\n|---|---|\n| `tenants.json` | 128 KiB, version 1, at most 64 tenants |\n| `fpfn-reports.json` | 4 MiB, version 1 |\n| `appearance.json` | 4 KiB |\n| A credentials item | 64 KiB |\n| A cases item | 256 KiB, at most 32 cases |\n| A history item | 8 MiB, archive and snapshots version 1 |\n| An Academy record | 16 MiB, at most 4,096 entries per account |\n\nOver a limit is `tooLarge`, another version is `unsupportedVersion`, and\nunreadable or unauthenticated data is `corrupt`. A selected category that\nis missing, unreadable or denied is shown blocked with its reason: nothing\nis silently skipped.\n\n**Two steps.**\n- Inspecting reads files only and counts what they show. What only a\n  Keychain read could tell is shown as not yet counted.\n- Planning reads protected items only for the selected categories, and\n  only for v1 tenants given a target.\n\n**Mapping.**\n\n| v1 | Imported | Not imported |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tenants.json` tenant | A new keyless tenant (label and TSG ID, C02's validation), or the operator's choice of an existing tenant with the same TSG ID | Environment, storage mode, credential reference, Gateway key references and preferences |\n| `credentials-{ref}` | Only the scan endpoint, as a destination: a region's origin, or a plain HTTPS origin | Every secret field (decoded only into a one-field structure), other endpoints. An unmappable or unreadable endpoint leaves the default region, and the item says why |\n| `appearance.json` | Light, dark or system | Theme family, time zone |\n| `navigation.json` | — | Not read (no v2 equivalent; shown) |\n| `cases-{tenant}` | Cases, rebound to the v2 tenant and validated (D038) | Session-only tenants (none stored), accounts v1 was retiring |\n| `fpfn-reports.json` | Reports | A sub-request beyond v2's range (counted) |\n| `academy-local/…` with its key | Unsaved drafts of the signed-in account, with v1's draft version as base | Downloads (Academy gives them again), saved or conflicting attempts |\n| `api-client/` | Recent collections, as C14's adoption adds them | Private environments: adopted through the API Client (C14). Secrets, drafts, history |\n| `harness-history-{ref}` | Each conversation's transcript, inspect-only | v1's engine replay, options, credential references. Approvals are shown declined, \"not transferred\" |\n\nNo credential, session, sign-in or approval is ever transferred:\n- imported tenants start with every capability missing;\n- Academy drafts need the same account signed in (its digest equals v1's\n  folder name; signed out is `signInRequired`, another account is\n  `accountMismatch`);\n- an existing tenant can be a target only when its TSG ID equals v1's.\n\n**Imported conversations.**\n- Each is saved with `origin: importedV1`, no restore point, and a binding\n  that can never equal a live one.\n- It reads `imported`: it can be read and exported, never continued.\n- Its scope is noted before its first write like any history save, and the\n  16-conversation cap holds (`destinationFull`; nothing is evicted).\n\n**Review.**\n- Each item is `ready`, `alreadyPresent` (v2 holds exactly v1's content\n  under the same identity), `conflict` (v2 holds different content; it is\n  never overwritten) or `blocked` with its reason.\n- Items show how many entries are new, present, in conflict or not\n  imported.\n- A review is used once. New tenants get their identity at review, so the\n  review, the receipt and a resumed import name the same tenant.\n\n**Receipts: the import's commit record.**\n- An import writes a receipt in two copies, `<data root>/imports/<id>.json`\n  and then `<data root>/imports.copy/<id>.json`: each 0600, replaced\n  atomically, and synced with its directory. A note is recorded only when\n  both copies and their directories are synced; anything less is\n  `notRecorded`, and nothing is written on it. A receipt is read from its\n  first copy, or from its second when the first is damaged or its folder\n  cannot be listed (the second is written last, so it never runs ahead of\n  the first). A missing folder holds no receipts; one that exists but\n  cannot be listed (or an entry of which cannot be read) is not taken for\n  empty, and a missing folder never stands in for an unreadable one.\n- The commit binding: while a receipt has not settled, every note also\n  writes a copy of it into the saved document (`openImports`), after both\n  files; settling removes the copy. A note is recorded only once that save\n  is confirmed durable. At launch, an unsettled receipt its files no longer\n  give (both damaged, or neither folder listable) is taken from the\n  document, so what it names stays hidden and its rollback runs; a copy\n  whose files say it settled is dropped (the files are newer). Losing the\n  document itself already makes local storage unavailable, and then\n  nothing local is shown or written. The field is present only while an\n  import is unsettled.\n- A document copy is checked as a receipt file is, when the document is\n  loaded: the current receipt schema, a valid ID named once, at most\n  `MAX_RECEIPTS` copies, and unsettled (the field's invariant). Any other\n  copy makes the document inconsistent, so it loads as damaged: nothing\n  in it is shown, published or saved, no rollback is driven by it, and the\n  document, the receipt files and the owner stores stay as they were for\n  the operator.\n- Inside the section that makes each owner write (the local-data writer\n  with the placement lock for cases and reports, the API Client's lane, the\n  saved document for tenants and appearance, the account's offline store\n  for drafts), the item is first noted `writing` with exactly the entries\n  it is about to add: only what that owner does not hold at that moment.\n  No other writer comes between the note and the write. The item is then\n  noted `applied` with the entries its owner confirms it added (`written`,\n  each with its content hash), kept apart from what it intended.\n- What an import adds is marked as its own where the owner allows: a new\n  tenant's identity is issued at review; a conversation is saved with\n  `origin: importedV1` and only while its scope holds no record of that\n  ID; a draft only while its attempt has no draft, with the receipt's ID\n  in it. A matching hash alone never makes an entry the import's.\n- Until the receipt is noted `applied`, every entry it names is hidden\n  from every view (tenants, appearance, the case library, reports, the\n  Harness history list, the API Client's recent list, Academy's kept\n  drafts and their reads) and the views show the previous generation. The\n  `applied` note is the commit: the receipt and its entries are published\n  together, in one step. Nothing it names gives authority meanwhile either:\n  its new tenant cannot be selected or be the active one, is not offered\n  as an existing target, and its conversation cannot be opened or resumed\n  by its ID.\n- Intents that write or export what an import writes wait (`busy`) while\n  an import or its rollback runs and until every receipt has settled\n  (`applied` or `rolledBack`): a rollback that could not finish (its\n  owner refused, at launch or later) keeps its receipt unsettled and what\n  it names hidden, and the wait lasts until a retried rollback settles it.\n  Nothing else can therefore write an entry the receipt names, so treating\n  an item that stopped while `writing` as having written its entries\n  never claims another writer's work.\n- A settled receipt readable in neither file, or receipt folders that\n  cannot be listed with nothing standing in, leave the inventory unknown\n  (what they named is committed or undone, so nothing is staged by them).\n  The app fails closed all the same: the same intents wait, no new import\n  or Import Again starts, and the import view names the lost record or\n  says the folders cannot be read, by alert. Restoring access and\n  reopening the app lists them again (a launch rollback's own writes\n  restore its folders' permissions, and the inventory is read again when\n  it ends). A lost record is released only by the operator removing it\n  (`forgetV1ImportReceipt`), which leaves whatever that import wrote as it\n  is and no longer roll-backable.\n- An import is whole or nothing. An item that cannot be written (its\n  source changed, its owner refused, its note was not recorded) rolls the\n  whole import back at once, without asking.\n- An import the app ended during is rolled back at the next launch, before\n  any intent is admitted: what it wrote, and what an item noted `writing`\n  may have written, is removed where v2 holds exactly that. The receipt\n  then reads undone (`interrupted`), and Import Again (`resumeV1Import`)\n  reads v1 again, requires the same bytes, and writes every item anew,\n  again only what v2 does not hold.\n- At most eight receipts are kept. A new import waits while one is\n  unsettled or eight are kept, so the ability to roll back is never\n  dropped silently.\n- Reset waits for an import or rollback under way, then removes receipts\n  with the data they describe.\n\nHow this meets the import contract of DESIGN §3 (\"reencrypt selected work\ninto a new v2 generation … atomically commit … preserve the previous\ngeneration for rollback … an interrupted import cannot expose a partially\ncommitted generation\"): v2 has no single store to swap, so each owner keeps\nits own sealed files and the receipt is the import's manifest. The staged\nentries are written through each owner but are not part of any view until\nthe receipt commits, and commit publication is one step; the previous\ngeneration is everything v2 held, which the import never overwrites (a\ndifferent entry under the same identity is a conflict); and an interrupted\nimport is rolled back before anything can see or build on it.\n\n**Writes go through each owner.**\n- Tenants and appearance: the saved document.\n- Cases and reports: the local-data writer that library and report\n  writes, tenant deletion and reset take, under the placement lock. An\n  open library or book is changed in memory and written as its newest\n  generation; one that opened during the write gets the same change in\n  memory, and one still loading reads again.\n- Drafts: the planned account's own offline store, only while that\n  account is signed in (another account, or none, is `accountMismatch` or\n  `signInRequired`, at review, at write and at rollback).\n- Collections: the API Client's lane.\n- Conversations: an admitted history save.\n\n**Rollback** (after the native confirmation for an applied import; at\nonce for one that failed or that the app ended during):\n- Its entries are hidden while it runs and go together.\n- Removes each entry only while v2 still holds exactly what the receipt\n  wrote, checked inside the owner's own section (a draft only for its\n  account, with the receipt's mark; a conversation only with its import\n  origin). One changed since is kept (`keptChanged`), one already removed\n  is `gone`.\n- Tenants go last, through Delete Tenant, and only while nothing else of\n  them exists: no credential, sign-in, project, server, case, report or\n  conversation.\n- A rollback that cannot finish (its note not recorded, or an entry it\n  cannot check now, such as a draft while another account is signed in)\n  stays unsettled and hidden; it finishes when asked again or at the next\n  launch.\n\n**Out of scope here:** private API Client environments (C14's adoption\nkeeps them); v1 settings with no v2 equivalent; live qualification against\na real v1 profile (C25).\n\n## 4. Managed workbench execution\n\nKeep RequestDocument semantics and immediate React editor truth separate from\nexecution permission. Send captures fresh complete input; native admission resolves\nvariables and references, validates method/URL/headers/body and constructs one\nimmutable effective request before attaching managed secrets.\n\nThe credential class is explicit: tenant-managed scan/management/Gateway grants,\nworkbench-owned secrets, or anonymous. Never infer the class from redactions.\nReplace v1's trust in the editable `prisma-airs-scm` collection ID: no imported\nID, request file, tenant reference or metadata flag grants access to tenant\nsecrets. Native authority registers explicit tenant/workspace capabilities for\nthe selected operation. Changing a collection ID cannot create that registration.\nClassify every requested tenant reference as managed before resolving it,\nindependent of collection provenance. In particular, admit a managed OAuth token\ndestination before obtaining client secrets; edited token_url variables cannot\nsend an existing grant to a new origin. Legitimate endpoint changes follow the\nexplicit profile/capability destination-review flow, invalidating old authority.\nKnown AIRS operations use a versioned method/path/destination catalogue:\n\n- Known reads: admit only with their actual capability and approved destination.\n- Known mutations: require a Configuration review of the exact effective request.\n- Unclassified managed requests, including unknown reads: deny without dispatch;\n  do not offer a renderer 'override' or blindly review an unknown operation.\n- Classification also checks registered Prisma origin/method/path independent of\n  credential class. Manually entered workbench credentials cannot bypass review\n  for known configuration mutations; unknown operations at registered managed\n  origins are denied until catalogued. Redaction is not classification.\n- Generic HTTP to other destinations with workbench-owned credentials/anonymous\n  access retains operator-directed behavior and cannot borrow tenant credentials.\n  Arbitrary third-party proxy semantics cannot be inferred universally; this rule\n  covers native-managed destinations and explicitly registered Prisma origins.\n\nA workbench review includes expanded URL/query, method, material headers, body/\nfile hashes, cookie snapshot, resolved variable revision, credential revision,\ndestination and resource/action classification. Secret values are never included\nin the review/model/log projection. Dispatch rechecks all bindings; changed\ninput/files/environment/credentials/cookies/context requires a new review.\nReject ambiguous path normalization, encoded separators/traversal, duplicate auth\nheaders, URL user-info and attacker-controlled managed proxy/Host overrides.\n\nDisable automatic redirects for managed requests in the first implementation.\nShow the 3xx result; following it is a new explicitly prepared/admitted request,\nincluding same-origin redirects. Workbench-owned redirects retain bounded generic\nHTTP behavior with explicit credential/cookie origin rules. This is stricter than\nv1's redactions-based cross-origin redirect guard and must be disclosed/tested.\n\nRemove v1's automatic post-401 resend for all workbench requests. Token refresh\nmay prepare a new explicit Send; it never repeats the customer operation. Lost\nresponses/timeouts remain uncertain after dispatch. No auto-replay on reconnect,\ncredential refresh, history replay, imported requests or engine suggestion.\nThe 193 catalogue entries (81 mutation-method specifications) are metadata parity,\nnot a claim of 193 dedicated workflows or live qualification.\n\n{\n  \"command_without_prompt\": [\n    \"codex\",\n    \"--no-daemon\",\n    \"-a\",\n    \"never\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"hooks\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"plugins\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"apps\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"remote_plugin\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"browser_use\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"browser_use_external\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"in_app_browser\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"computer_use\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"image_generation\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"multi_agent\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"skill_search\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"skill_mcp_dependency_install\",\n    \"--disable\",\n    \"unbounded_connection_retries\",\n    \"exec\",\n    \"-s\",\n    \"read-only\",\n    \"--skip-git-repo-check\",\n    \"--ephemeral\",\n    \"--ignore-user-config\",\n    \"--ignore-rules\",\n    \"-m\",\n    \"gpt-6.1-sol\",\n    \"--json\",\n    \"--color\",\n    \"never\",\n    \"-c\",\n    \"web_search=\\\"disabled\\\"\",\n    \"-c\",\n    \"model_reasoning_effort=\\\"xhigh\\\"\",\n    \"--output-schema\",\n    \"docs/architecture/review-verdict.schema.json\",\n    \"-o\",\n    \"/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2-current/docs/planning/reviews/P09/attempt-02/verdict-returned.json\"\n  ],\n  \"model_requested\": \"gpt-6.1-sol\",\n  \"harness_version_observed\": \"codex-cli 0.166.0\",\n  \"request_sha256\": \"866c77d4bd484cf9878cfc9ccd89d808153e6c3bb9738f14ca572eeff0960ec9\",\n  \"schema_sha256\": \"fa5ddc9b8371a97cdfb8055387e31916fad002859f88fc6f21340844ee2a1875\",\n  \"feature_probe_sha256\": \"bb4d01d583993c8e3c431e0437092e6c288e9b5da919595063853d37e2d89862\",\n  \"restricted_features_observed\": {\n    \"hooks\": \"false\",\n    \"plugins\": \"false\",\n    \"apps\": \"false\",\n    \"remote_plugin\": \"false\",\n    \"browser_use\": \"false\",\n    \"browser_use_external\": \"false\",\n    \"in_app_browser\": \"false\",\n    \"computer_use\": \"false\",\n    \"image_generation\": \"false\",\n    \"multi_agent\": \"false\",\n    \"skill_search\": \"false\",\n    \"skill_mcp_dependency_install\": \"false\",\n    \"unbounded_connection_retries\": \"false\"\n  },\n  \"limits\": \"CLI arguments and feature probe establish requested isolation; inspect actual run events and reviewer limitations. No provider/model authorship or review pass is inferred.\"\n}\n#!/bin/bash\n# P09 check runs with a source-bound record. Usage: run-checks.sh <attempt>\n# Runs on the approved toolchain (rustup 1.99.0, node 24.21.0, repo pnpm).\nset -o pipefail\nATTEMPT=${1:-attempt-01}\ncd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../../../../..\"\nexport PATH=\"$PWD/.tools/bin:$HOME/.cargo/bin:$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\"\nunset RUSTFLAGS CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS CARGO_TARGET_DIR\n# Timing-sensitive suites (4 s \"eventually\" waits, engine bootstraps) flake when ten test threads\n# per binary share this Mac with the owner's browser and a second session; four threads per\n# binary keep the same tests and assertions, only less contention. Recorded in the run record.\nexport RUST_TEST_THREADS=4\nR=docs/planning/evidence/P09/runs\nE=$R/$ATTEMPT\nif [ -e \"$E\" ] || [ -e \"$R/run-$ATTEMPT.json\" ]; then\n  echo \"Refusing to overwrite retained P09 check evidence: $ATTEMPT; choose a fresh attempt.\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\nmkdir -p \"$E\"\n# Flake logs are same-input retries of this run only: earlier runs' logs are removed first.\nrm -f $E/core-fixtures.flake-*.log $E/host.flake-*.log\nBEFORE=$($R/hash-inputs.sh \"$PWD\" $E/inputs-before.sha256) || { echo \"input inventory failed; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\n[ -n \"$BEFORE\" ] || { echo \"input inventory produced no digest; run invalid\"; exit 2; }\n# Configuration and build flags the controller fingerprints: itemised and hashed together.\nBUILD_FLAGS=$(shasum -a 256 docs/architecture/checks.json docs/architecture/native-checks.json docs/architecture/toolchain.json docs/architecture/budgets.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.fixture.conf.json apps/desktop/src-tauri/capabilities/*.json rust-toolchain.toml 2>/dev/null | shasum -a 256 | cut -d' ' -f1)\nexport BUILD_FLAGS\nrun() { local name=$1; shift; local log=$E/$name.log; echo \"\\$ $*\" > \"$log\"; \"$@\" >> \"$log\" 2>&1; local rc=$?; echo \"EXIT $rc\" >> \"$log\"; echo \"$name exit=$rc\"; echo $rc > $E/$name.exit; }\nrun rustfmt cargo fmt --all -- --check\nrun clippy cargo clippy --locked -p desktop-core -p desktop-host -p xtask --features test-fixtures --all-targets -- -D warnings\nrun clippy-default cargo clippy --locked --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\nrun notices cargo run --locked -q -p xtask -- notices --check\nrun bindings cargo run --locked -q -p xtask -- bindings --check\nrun contracts-typecheck pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop-contracts exec tsc --noEmit\nrun desktop-typecheck pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec tsc --noEmit\nrun vitest pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec vitest run --config vitest.config.ts\nrun vitest-truth pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec vitest run --config vitest.truth.config.ts\nrun core-lib cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --lib\nrun core-fixtures cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures\n# A timing-sensitive test (Harness-engine turns, or the transport's connection-release\n# check) can flake under the full-suite load: the exact same\n# command is retried on the same frozen inputs, every failed run kept as evidence.\nfor try in 1 2; do\n  if [ \"$(cat $E/core-fixtures.exit)\" != \"0\" ]; then\n    cp $E/core-fixtures.log $E/core-fixtures.flake-$try.log\n    run core-fixtures cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures\n  fi\ndone\n# The chunk's registered fixture command (docs/architecture/checks.json P09), exactly as the manifest runs it.\nrun fixture-command cargo test --locked -p desktop-core --features test-fixtures --test ui_v2_closure --test ui_v2_shell_port --test ui_v2_home --test ui_v2_inbox_port --test ui_v2_harness_port --test ui_v2_admin --test ui_v2_api --test ui_v2_academy --test ui_v2_wizard\nrun host cargo test --locked -p desktop-host --features test-fixtures\n# The host's Academy device-flow tests (browser_callback.rs, outside this chunk) wait 5 s for a\n# state and flake under load: the same command is retried on the same frozen inputs, every\n# failed run kept as evidence.\nfor try in 1 2; do\n  if [ \"$(cat $E/host.exit)\" != \"0\" ]; then\n    cp $E/host.log $E/host.flake-$try.log\n    run host cargo test --locked -p desktop-host --features test-fixtures\n  fi\ndone\nrun xtask-lib cargo test --locked -p xtask --lib\nrun shell-controller cargo test --locked -p xtask --features test-fixtures --test shell_controller\nrun desktop-build pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop build\nrun l19-offline cargo test --locked -p xtask --features test-fixtures --test controlled_live --test live_run\n# Offline unittest only: retained/synthetic AX settlement and tempfile budget\n# ledgers. Imports pure helpers; never calls the live driver's guarded main(),\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
